From 4736176f17b2734d372591f048c674b1ebccea85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pasarguard <dev@pasarguard.local>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 04:11:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(clashapi): let every supported protocol take users
 at runtime
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The endpoint was written generic; only its dispatch knew hysteria2. The other
six inbounds could not have their users replaced at all, because none of them
exposed the UpdateUsers their service already had — it was called once, inside
the constructor, and never again.

vless, vmess, trojan, tuic and hysteria gain one; shadowsocks already had it.

Each carries the same hazard hysteria2 did: the inbound keeps its own copy of
the user list and resolves a connecting client's name by indexing it with the id
the service returns. Those are two halves of one fact. Swapping them separately
bills traffic arriving mid-swap to whoever now sits at that index — which is
exactly what happened in testing, where one user's download was recorded against
a stranger. Every patch swaps both under a single write lock, and bounds-checks
the lookup: an id can arrive from a connection authenticated against a longer
list, and indexing past the end panics the process.

Three things only the compiler caught, all in tuic: it names its service field
`server` where the others use `service`, its UpdateUsers takes [][16]byte rather
than []uuid.UUID, and it rejects an empty uuid before parsing — a check the
first draft dropped.
